From 2f1d79c94672527caf1a5ee3aa6d7b17ce01e6a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 14:54:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Gate tenant data behind identity, and stream chat
 responses

Every data-bearing endpoint was anonymous, so unauthenticated callers could
upgrade any account to the top paid tier, enumerate accounts by email, read and
export any conversation by session id, and list, download, or delete every
uploaded file. Entitlement checks keyed off a client-supplied X-Auricrux-Email
header, which a caller could forge to borrow another plan or simply omit to skip
metering and model gating altogether.

AuricruxSecurityOptions resolves one posture at startup. Open mode keeps the
historical shared-tenant behaviour for local development and the test host; it is
off in Production unless Security:AllowInsecureProduction is set, and the posture
is logged at boot. CallerContext derives the caller from a validated token when
authentication is configured, honours the email header only in open mode, and
partitions storage by a hashed tenant key. The public tenant keeps the existing
on-disk layout so single-tenant deployments need no data migration. Plan changes
require an administrator via a constant-time X-Auricrux-Admin-Key check or an
admin role claim. Stateless inference stays open by default, since closing it is
a deployment decision rather than a security fix; Security:RequireAuthForInference
closes it.

Chat also only ever returned a completed response, because every Ollama call set
stream=false, so a Deep-mode answer showed nothing until it finished. That was the
most visible behavioural gap against Copilot, Gemini, Grok, and Claude, which all
render tokens as they arrive. POST /api/chat/stream emits Server-Sent Events,
leading with a sources event so citations render immediately, then token events,
then a done event carrying the interaction id for feedback.

Enum values sent as JSON strings were rejected outright, so the wire format the
MAUI client actually sends and the contract the README documents both returned
400 and only the undocumented numeric form worked; JsonStringEnumConverter now
accepts strings and numbers alike. Uploads are checked against an extension
allow-list and a size limit enforced while copying rather than trusted from the
declared length, with partial files removed on rejection. The workspace root
check compares against root plus a separator so a sibling directory cannot pass
it. The CSP drops 'unsafe-eval', which Blazor Server does not need, and adds
object-src, base-uri, and form-action; API responses are marked no-store.
